menu "Solana Scavenger Hunt Relay"

    config RELAY_WIFI_SSID
        string "WiFi SSID"
        default ""
        help
            Network the relay joins to reach the webhook. WPA2-PSK.

    config RELAY_WIFI_PASSWORD
        string "WiFi password"
        default ""
        help
            Leave empty for an open network.

    config RELAY_WIFI_STA_MAC
        string "Custom WiFi STA MAC (xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx, empty = chip default)"
        default ""
        help
            Override the WiFi station MAC, e.g. for a MAC-whitelisted IoT
            network. Empty means keep the chip efuse MAC. Applied with
            esp_wifi_set_mac() before wifi start.

            The Down-button MAC editor (NVS-persisted) takes precedence over
            this when set, using the event's fixed 24:0A:C4:C8:46 prefix
            and a selected last byte from A0 through B3.

    config RELAY_WEBHOOK_URL
        string "Webhook URL"
        default "https://relay-server-pied.vercel.app/api/open"
        help
            Every Solana Scavenger Hunt request is POSTed here verbatim, and the reply is
            broadcast straight back to the badge. The relay never parses the
            payload in either direction.

            This lives on the relay rather than in the badge's request so that
            repointing the server doesn't mean reflashing every badge, and so a
            stranger in BLE range can't make the relay POST to an arbitrary host.

    config RELAY_BOX_ID
        string "Box id"
        default "relay-01"
        help
            Identifies this station. Broadcast in the body of every idle
            beacon; the badge puts it in the request JSON, so it reaches the
            webhook as the "box" field.

            This is the fallback boot selection: the Up-button ID editor
            steps through the fixed station-slug list at runtime and the
            choice persists in NVS. Set it to the slug for where this box
            lives.

            Max 39 characters, to match the badge's box id buffer.

    config RELAY_SELFTEST_ON_BOOT
        bool "POST a test request at boot"
        default y
        help
            Sends one canned request to the webhook during startup and logs the
            result. Lets you verify WiFi, DNS, TLS, and the server before any
            BLE traffic exists. Safe to leave on.

    config RELAY_SUPABASE_URL
        string "Supabase URL for heartbeats"
        default "https://qoouxoeavyuokuscvxff.supabase.co"
        help
            Project URL for the 3-minute heartbeat (touch_relay_heartbeat
            RPC). Same project as the relay server's box_decisions table.

    config RELAY_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY
        string "Supabase anon key for heartbeats"
        default ""
        help
            Publishable anon key (Project Settings -> API). Only used to
            call the touch_relay_heartbeat() RPC; the heartbeat tables
            have no public access. Empty disables the heartbeat task.

    config RELAY_MAC_EDIT_ENABLE
        bool "Down-button WiFi MAC editor (NVS-persisted override)"
        default n
        help
            Lets the Down button set a custom STA-MAC last byte (A0..B3),
            persisted in NVS and taking precedence over RELAY_WIFI_STA_MAC.
            Off while venue debugging: the box then uses RELAY_WIFI_STA_MAC
            (or the chip MAC when empty) with no runtime override.

    config RELAY_HEARTBEAT_ENABLE
        bool "Send a Supabase heartbeat every 3 minutes"
        default y
        help
            POSTs this box's id to Supabase on a loop. A pg_cron watchdog
            Slacks when a box goes stale (see the _heartbeats migration).
            Needs SUPABASE_ANON_KEY above; without it the task exits.

    config RELAY_SCREEN_ENABLE
        bool "LCD status screen (same panel as the badge)"
        default y
        help
            Shows what the relay is doing on the badge-family LCD
            (ST7789 320x240 over SPI, rendered with LVGL — the same
            graphics system as the badge). The display is status-only:
            without it the relay runs headless and every screen call
            is a no-op.

endmenu
